{
  "file": "crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs",
  "item": "impl[StoredGraph/Fingerprinting]/fn[fingerprint]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "These storage and output-decoding checks use records with no content\n dependencies. The required fingerprint callback must remain unused:\n consulting graph contents would mask a storage-layer or decoding error.\n Other domain fixtures exercise tracked fingerprint observations."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs` `impl[StoredGraph/Fingerprinting]/fn[fingerprint]` is valid.\n\n- **Form:** It is a `cite`, which is the role a built-in coverage exception takes. It sits on an explicit function with a body. The explanation is in fenced citation commentary, as the built-in text requires. It is not an ordinary comment or a plain TODO.\n- **Substance:** The commentary gives a real reason for the function's zero execution. `StoredGraph` is a storage-only consumer, and `Fingerprinting` requires the callback. The function is a deliberate guard: it panics, so any content resolution would fail the test loudly. That keeps graph content from masking a storage-layer or output-decoding error. The commentary also says other fixtures cover tracked fingerprint observations. This is a design reason, not just \"it is hard to cover\".\n- **Agreement with source:** `fn[fingerprint]` does panic with a message that matches the commentary. In the validator test, `malformed_records_are_storage_errors_and_current_bad_outputs_are_output_errors`, `StoredReview::execute` returns a fixed string and never touches graph contents. The record that `verify` stores therefore has no dependencies, so `check` and `verify` never need to fingerprint. The other uses of `StoredGraph` call `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` directly. The `record()` fixture does contain `Dependency::Node` entries. It is only stored and loaded through the storage layer, never validated, so nothing consults `fingerprint`. The \"no content dependencies\" claim therefore holds for the checks that go through the validator.\n- **Redundancy:** The item carries no `impl` or `test` reference to the same name, so the citation is not redundant.\n- **Independent assessment:** The built-in text also makes the exception depend on a current accepted independent assessment. That is outside this item's text, and this review is that assessment.\n\nI found no gap.",
